## Architecture overview
3-5 sentences: the overall shape of the system — application layer, AI layer, data layer — and how the modules below fit into it. Plain language an owner can follow, precise enough that an engineer nods.

## Module specifications
One ### subsection PER MODULE, in the decomposition's order. Each subsection covers, as short labeled lines (restating the module's "spec" and "tech" facts — do not invent beyond them; omit any line whose underlying data is missing):
- **Data model:** each entity from tech.data_model with its key fields, e.g. "Pre-Approval Request (patient, carrier, treatment codes, status, submitted_at)"
- **Key screens:** from the spec ("screens")
- **The AI agent:** one sentence of its purpose and model tier — or the line "No AI component — deliberately." when there is none. When there is one, follow with:
- **Agent brain:** the knowledge it is grounded in (tech.ai_agent.brain)
- **Agent tools:** each tool name and what calling it does (tech.ai_agent.tools)
- **Agent memory:** what it remembers and for how long
- **Guardrails:** what it must never do
- **Escalation:** the trigger that hands off to a human, and to whom
- **How we evaluate it:** the checks before trusting it (tech.ai_agent.evaluation)
- **APIs & events:** from tech.apis
- **Connects to:** tech.integration_details — each system and what data flows which direction
- **Security:** tech.security notes for this module
- **Build sequence:** tech.build_sequence as a numbered inline list — the order to stand this module up
- **Done when:** tech.done_when — the checks that prove this module is finished
- **Build notes:** 1-2 sentences of real engineering judgement: the hard part, the risk, or the thing to get right first

## Bringing it to life yourself
This plan is complete enough to execute without us — this section is the honest guide for a client who takes it to their own team. Cover, as short labeled bullets:
- **Team shape:** the smallest team that can build this (roles, not names — e.g. one full-stack developer plus one AI engineer; adjust to THIS system's real complexity)
- **Build order:** the modules in dependency order, one line of reasoning each — which module's data the others need first
- **Buy, don't build:** the pieces to take off the shelf (messaging/SMS provider, calendaring, hosting, auth) rather than write — named as CATEGORIES with what to look for, never a specific vendor pitch
- **Proving the agents before launch:** how to use each module's evaluation checks to trust the AI before real customers touch it — human review loops first, thresholds before autonomy
- **The first thing to ship:** the smallest end-to-end slice that touches real users, and why starting there de-risks the rest

## Data, security & reliability
4-5 bullets, system-wide: where data lives, backups, privacy of client information, access control, uptime — plain language, no boilerplate that could describe any product.

